{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Good Deeds"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "A Clean Conscience"}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 13}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": " Express duty, advice, willingness, or intention to do something in spoken or written texts and in work and social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn11le13ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " Negatives with <span class=\"strong\">Must</span> and <span class=\"strong\">Have to</span>"}, {"type": "describer", "content": " The negatives of <span class=\"strong\">must</span> and <span class=\"strong\">have to</span> have somewhat different meanings than their affirmative counterparts. Let's look at the differences in meanings:"}, {"type": "text", "content": " <span class=\"strong\">Don't have to</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you want to talk about a situation in which you have the freedom to choose to do something, you use  <span class=\"strong\">don't have to</span>. There is no obligation, you are free to act as you will. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "  For example:<br/>  <span class=\"strong\"> You <span class=\"strong\">don't have to</span> attend the company Christmas party.</span> (You can choose to go or not to go)"}, {"type": "text", "content": " <span class=\"strong\">Must not</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you want to talk about something that is prohibited (strictly forbidden or unacceptable), you use  <span class=\"strong\">must not</span>. "}, {"type": "text", "content": " For example:<br/>   <span class=\"italic\"> Passengers <span class=\"strong\">must not</span> bring liquids onto the plane.<br/> You <span class=\"strong\">mustn't</span> enter a contract without reading it first.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Your conscience is the voice saying you mustn't do this, you cannot think that or you may not go there."}, {"type": "example", "content": "In this way of thinking, actions are either inherently right or wrong and an individual must distinguish between them. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "You don't have to find the \"right\" theory for all situations, but to see what insight each perspective on the path towards a clear consciousness."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " <span class=\"stronf\">Will</span> and <span class=\"strong\">Would</span> for Willingness and Intention"}, {"type": "describer", "content": " When you want to express intention to do something or a willingness to do something, you can use the modal verbs <span class=\"strong\">will</span> and <span class=\"strong\">would</span>."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We will choose our own path according to our personal judgment."}, {"type": "example", "content": "They are situations in which you are confronted with two imperfect choices, but you will choose only one. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you are a utilitarian, you will prioritize the consequences over the actions themselves.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use <span class=\"strong\">will</span> to talk about someone's willingness in the present moment to do something. <BR/> You can use <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> to talk about someone's UNwillingness in the present moment to do something. <BR/> When talking about the past, you can use <span class=\"strong\">would</span> to express that someone was generally willing to do things. <BR/> You can use <span class=\"strong\">wouldn't</span> to talk about UNwillingness in the past in both single situations and generally. <BR/>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Context is particularly important to understand the exact meaning of each verb."}, {"type": "text", "content": " For example:<br/>   <span class=\"italic\">Joanna <span class=\"strong\">will</span> translate for you - she speaks Japanese. </span>(willingness)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I'll do it - I don't mind.</span> (willingness)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">The workers <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> vote for me. </span>(willingness)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> be coming later. </span>(intention)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Would</span> you mind helping me, please? </span>(willingness)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I'll call you later. </span>(intention)"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "conscience"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the inner sense of what is right or wrong "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: How humans came to develop a conscience has been a subject of debate and research at least since Darwin's Origin of Species was published in 1859. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " These are words you use when you talk about moral dilemmas."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "judgment"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the ability to judge or make decisions"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We will choose our own path according to our personal judgment."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob10aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "imperfect"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not perfect; faulty in some way"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They are situations in which you are confronted with two imperfect choices, but you will choose only one."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob10aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "utilitarianism"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an ethical doctrine based on utility"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: One technique is to think about a problem in light of the theory of utilitarianism. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob10aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "deontology "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a form of ethics dealing with moral obligation and right action"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can also approach an ethical dilemma through deontology. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob10aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "inherently"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " existing within or describing something by its nature "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In this way of thinking, actions are either inherently right or wrong and an individual must distinguish between them. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le13ob10aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "With a partner, discuss the two ethical doctrines described in this lesson."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Choose a moral dilemma."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Give your opi