{"incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!"}}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "\nInstructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self Assessment.\n"}], "type": "post_instructions"}]}], "type": "scholar", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "id": 0, "stimuli": [{"contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "id": 0, "template_override": 1}, {"contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "engenn11le13ob10re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "", "id": 0, "image": "engenn11le13ob10re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "1", "text": "A Clear Conscience </br ></br >Remember that little voice inside your head telling you that you are thinking about doing wrong? It is the voice saying you mustn't do this, you cannot think that or you may not go there. It's trying to keep you out of danger and protect your place in the social environment.\nHow humans came to develop a conscience has been a subject of debate and research at least since Darwin's Origin of Species was published in 1859.  Although our inner consciousness may detect something is up, it can't always point us in the right direction. We will choose our own path according to our personal judgment.", "id": 1, "image": "engenn11le13ob10re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "43000", "text": "Ethical dilemmas are situations in which there is no clear \"right\" thing to do. They are situations in which you are confronted with two imperfect choices, but you will choose only one. Helpfully, there are techniques you can use to determine what you believe the best choice is.\nOne technique is to think about a problem in light of the theory of utilitarianism. This theory posits that the \"right\" thing is the one that provides the most good, or \u201cutility\" to society as a whole. In this way, ethics becomes a sort of equation. For example, in a situation where you were forced to make a decision between one person dying and many people dying, then you must let the one person die and allow the many to live.  If you are a utilitarian, you will prioritize the consequences over the actions themselves.", "id": 2, "image": "engenn11le13ob10re2img02.jpg"}, {"time": "101000", "text": "You can also approach an ethical dilemma through deontology.  In this way of thinking, actions are either inherently right or wrong and an individual must distinguish between them. To do this, an individual follows a set of rules. If you believe in deontology, you will assess the value of particular actions less than their consequences.<br/>\nWhile pure utilitarianism and pure deontology are two sides of a spectrum, most theories of right and wrong are some mix between the two. You don't have to find the \"right\" theory for all situations, but to see what insight each perspective has on the path towards a clear consciousness.", "id": 3, "image": "engenn11le13ob10re2img03.jpg"}], "coverimg": "engenn11le13ob10re2img01.jpg"}}], "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "id": 1, "_data": [], "title": ""}]}]}